From 9d66e093d8207ec0331e26d8c6168631a09db369 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 21:20:01 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/15] Say when the canvas reaches the screen with fewer bits
 per channel than the document
Forwarded: no, Krita's README sets a moratorium on AI-assisted contributions; carried in the edition's fork,
 https://invent.kde.org/danielcamposramos/krita

A 16-bit or float document can reach the compositor at 10 bits per
channel, or in half float, when the graphics stack offers no deeper
canvas surface: the picture is quantized there with nothing said.

When the surface that reaches the compositor (the canvas layer, or the
window where there is none) is first shown, and whenever the image's
colour space changes, its depth is compared with the document's, up to
16 bits per channel (what a display link carries at most): fewer bits,
or half float (about 11 significant bits) for a 16-bit integer document,
is said on the canvas and in the log. The component type of the layer's
EGL configuration tells half float from 16-bit integer
(EGL_EXT_pixel_format_float).

Where the person chose a shallower canvas surface in Settings > Configure
Krita > Color Management, the note says that setting is the cause rather
than the graphics stack: KisOpenGL keeps the depth the setting asked for
at this start (it applies from the next start). A 32-bit float document
is called 32 bits in the note.

